  The stated motivation also doesn't hold up on inspection: the timestamps in [38;5;153m_events[39m are never logged or exposed. I
  grepped the repo — nothing reads [38;5;153m_events[39m, and there's no logging anywhere in the project. So there is nothing
  currently to line up with wall-clock log timestamps.

  If the real goal is correlating limiter decisions with gateway logs, the fix is to log a [38;5;153mtime.time()[39m value at the
  decision point and keep the internal window on [38;5;153mtime.monotonic()[39m. Want me to add that?

  [1m3. Pluggable storage backend — pushing back on YAGNI grounds.[0m There's no Redis in the project (no dependency in
  [38;5;153mpyproject.toml[39m, no reference anywhere in the tree), and [38;5;153mSlidingWindowLimiter[39m has exactly one caller-facing method. An
  abstract backend interface added now would be designed against a guessed Redis implementation rather than a real one —
  and the real one differs structurally, since a shared Redis window needs atomicity (a Lua script or
  [38;5;153mZADD[39m/[38;5;153mZREMRANGEBYSCORE[39m in a transaction) that a [38;5;153mdeque[39m-shaped interface won't express. The prep work would most likely
  need rewriting when Redis actually lands.

  Extracting the interface at that point is a small, mechanical change against a known second implementation. If Redis
  is committed and imminent rather than hypothetical, tell me and I'll do it — but I'd want the actual Redis
  requirements first, not a placeholder abstraction.

